{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785651557,"nonce":"llfjSNci3+uCEvKoa0kmpxaE/MI=","title":"Established 2026-08-02 by reading the code end to end. A step with multiple `needs:` sees ONE /workspace built by sequentially replaying each parent's snapshot over the same directory in declared `needs:` order — a per-path union with **last-root-wins on file collisions**. Deterministic (order comes from the Run's pinned IR, not completion order). Directories union correctly; there is no tree-merge function at all, so the merkle \"one subtree replaces the other\" hazard does NOT occur.","message":"**The rule is asserted only by code comments that cite ADR-0007 for a rule ADR-0007 does not contain.** ADR-0007:25 says a step inherits \"the merged content-addressed workspace of its needs\" and never defines merge order, conflict resolution, or union-vs-replace. A grep of all 65 ADRs finds nothing on workspace fan-in conflicts (0023's fan-in is scheduling; 0055's merge order is Pod specs). CONTEXT.md is silent too.\n\nWhere it actually lives: `workspace_inputs` returns Vec\u003cString\u003e (scarab-engine/src/lib.rs:962-971); order preserved through StepSpec.workspace_inputs and the WSFETCH_ROOTS env (executor-k8s:3171); the merge is a deliberately sequential loop in scarab-wsfetch.rs:292-306 calling `materialize` per root into the same target dir; `write_entry` unlinks-then-writes so a later root can replace a read-only file or symlink.\n\nThree defects, compounding:\n\n1. **Collisions are SILENT** — no log, no event, nothing in the UI. Two branches emitting different bytes at the same path resolve by declared order with no diagnostic anywhere.\n2. **`input_signature` SORTS its inputs (lib.rs:998) while materialization does not.** The two are documented as covering \"exactly the same set\" and are not the same *sequence*. Not reachable today (a Run's needs order is fixed by its pinned IR), but it is a latent cache-correctness bug: reordering `needs:` changes the bytes a step reads while leaving its skip signature identical.\n3. **No end-to-end test of a diamond with overlapping paths.** `server/tests/workspace_inputs.rs` drives a real {B,C}-\u003eD diamond but asserts only the LIST reaching the executor; `workspace-client/tests/service_roundtrip.rs:734` overlays two roots with a colliding file but hand-ingests the roots with no DAG. Nothing joins the two halves. The kind tier is single-input everywhere.\n\nInherent limit worth documenting rather than fixing: **deletions are not representable** across a fan-in. A file one branch deliberately removed reappears if the other branch still has it.\n\nDECIDED direction (keep last-wins; make it loud):\n- [ ] Write the semantics into ADR-0007 as an amendment: per-path union, last-root-in-declared-order wins per file, directories union, deletions not representable, order is the pinned IR's order and never completion order\n- [ ] A collision produces a diagnostic and an event — the materialiser knows it is overwriting; silence is the actual bug\n- [ ] Reconcile `input_signature` with materialisation order (the signature must be order-SENSITIVE, since order determines the bytes) or prove the divergence unreachable and pin that with a test\n- [ ] The missing test: a diamond DAG where both branches write the same path with different bytes, driven end to end, asserting which bytes win AND that the collision was reported\n- [ ] Note for the ADR-0062 feed: `ExportRequest.parent` is SINGULAR (export.rs:748, 819, 891; workspaced.rs:2464), so the lazy Export path cannot express fan-in as written. overlayfs multi-lower (`lowerdir=B:A`, leftmost wins) maps exactly onto these semantics at zero copy cost — make the contract take a LIST before building against the singular form","files":null}]}